  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 650 PM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost and Freeze headlines are in effect for portions of \nEastern Virginia and Northeast North Carolina late tonight \nthrough early tomorrow morning. Chilly weather continues through\nTuesday. \n\nUpdate: Based on the latest guidance, went ahead and replaced\nthe Frost Advisory with a Freeze Warning for Bertie, Gates, and\nSuffolk. The Frost Advisory was expanded to include inland\nCurrituck, Virginia Beach, and York. \n\nPrevious Discussion: Temperatures that were at or below \nfreezing were observed across the Richmond metro and points \nsouth resulting in the growing season being ended for portions \nof interior southeast VA and northeast NC. The season was \nalready over for the piedmont west of Richmond. Frost Advisories\nand Freeze Warnings will not be issued for these areas until \nthe season resumes in Spring 2027. \n\nEarly afternoon weather analysis indicates UL low pressure over New \nEngland with a strong attending trough that dips down toward the \nGulf. The trough axis swinging through the Mid-Atlantic today has \ntriggered broken-overcast mid level cloud cover across much of \nthe local FA. At the sfc, high pressure over the Mississippi \nRiver valley is trying to build east behind a sfc low that is \nwell offshore. NW winds are rather gusty as cold, dry air \ncontinues to advect into the area. Wind gusts between 20-30mph \nhave been observed today. Latest temps are in the upper 40s \nacross the far north and in the low 50s elsewhere. NBM bias \ncorrection has been a little overzealous as of late with \novernight lows, so did decide to go a few degrees warmer than \nthe NBM for the forecast. Nevertheless, clearing skies and \nlighter winds will still allow temps to drop to near or below \nfreezing away from the immediate coast. The Eastern Shore will \nface lingering cloud cover and winds of 5-10kt, so lows around \n40 forecast there. Widespread frost is expected for areas away \nfrom the immediate coast (excluding the Eastern Shore). A Frost \nAdvisory has been issued for late tonight and early tomorrow \nmorning for interior portions of eastern VA and northeast NC \nwhere the growing season has not technically ended. \n\nTomorrow and Monday will be quite similar to today as the overall as \nthe pattern will be slow to change. High pressure will slowly slide \neast and the UL will gradually weaken. Highs will be in the 50s and \nlows will be at or below freezing inland, mid-upper 30s at the \ncoast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Temperatures show a gradual warming trend mid-\nweek through the weekend. Remaining dry. \n\nHigh pressure remains largely in place through the end of the week. \nAloft, the trough looks to finally move offshore around mid week, \nthen a ridge builds in behind it. This will allow for a moderating \ntrend in temperatures through the weekend. Forecast highs are in the \nmid 60s for Wednesday, then upper 60s Thurs-Sat. Lows will likely \nstill drop into the 30s each morning. With dry high pressure in \ncharge, there doesn't look to be a relief from the dry spell anytime \nsoon.",
